## 8. Jenkins Execute Shell Script

Add the following script in the Jenkins shell execution step after the Maven build.

This assumes Jenkins and Docker are running on the same EC2 machine.

```bash
#!/bin/bash
set -e

IMAGE="manoj65/projectdeployment"
TAG="27"

echo "===== CHECK WAR FILE ====="
ls -lh webapp/target/*.war

echo "===== DOCKER LOGIN ====="
echo "$DOCKER_PASS" | docker login \
  -u "$DOCKER_USER" \
  --password-stdin

echo "===== BUILD DOCKER IMAGE ====="
cd webapp

docker build \
  -t "$IMAGE:$TAG" \
  -t "$IMAGE:latest" .

echo "===== PUSH IMAGE TO DOCKER HUB ====="
docker push "$IMAGE:$TAG"
docker push "$IMAGE:latest"

echo "===== REMOVE OLD CONTAINER ====="
docker rm -f webapp || true

echo "===== DEPLOY NEW CONTAINER ====="
docker run -d \
  --name webapp \
  --restart unless-stopped \
  -p 8090:8080 \
  "$IMAGE:$TAG"

echo "===== VERIFY CONTAINER ====="
docker ps

echo "===== DOCKER LOGOUT ====="
docker logout

echo "===== DEPLOYMENT COMPLETED ====="
```

Important:

- The Dockerfile must be inside `webapp`.
- The WAR file must exist before Docker build.
- Configure the shell step to run only after a successful Maven build.
- The script uses image tag `27`. Change it to `28`, `29`, etc. for future versioned releases.
- The `latest` tag is updated whenever a new image is pushed with that tag.

